oledb32: convert VARIANT to DBTYPE_I8 and DBTYPE_BOOL, so a natively bound 64-bit or boolean column can be read

Power BI Desktop reads a visual's data through the MSOLAP OLE DB provider.  The engine answers the
query, and then the read of the result rowset fails inside the provider's native wrapper, at the
last statement of `MsolapWrapper::CChapterBulkRowset<CDynamicAccessor>::MoveNext`:
`IRowset::GetData`.  The app shows the visual as

    Error fetching data for this visual

and its own trace carries the provider's HResult `0x80040E21` (`DB_E_ERRORSINCOMMAND`) with
`dwMinor = 0` and the provider's fallback message (`Error Code = 0x80040E21, External Code =
0x00000000:.`), i.e. the app cannot say more than "the provider refused the read".

Reproduced without the app, against the engine the app itself starts (`EVALUATE ROW("i8", 1)`, Power
BI's own command property set, and a natively bound accessor; probe `tools/winapi/msolapprobe.c`):

    IRowset::GetData -> hr=0x80040E21  (FAILED)
    column[1] name=[i8] wType=0x0014 size=8 prec=19 scale=255 flags=0x00000050
    IErrorInfo::GetSource="Microsoft OLE DB Provider for Analysis Services."

The trigger is the *requested type of the binding* and nothing else.  The same statement with
`--force-type 0x15` (DBTYPE_UI8 - the same eight bytes) returns `S_OK` with value `1`, as do
`DBTYPE_I4`, `DBTYPE_VARIANT`, `DBTYPE_BSTR` and `DBTYPE_WSTR`; the values themselves are
irrelevant (`0`, `-1` and `1234567890123` fail alike); a `DBTYPE_BOOL` column fails the same way;
`R8`, `DATE` and `WSTR` columns read fine; neither fetch size nor `cbMaxLen` nor the interface
`Execute` was asked for changes anything.  Without Power BI's property set the same inability is
reported as `DB_S_ERRORSOCCURRED` plus a per-column `DBSTATUS_E_BADACCESSOR`.  The identical
command on a Windows guest returns `S_OK` for the same probe binary, so this is a Wine divergence,
not a document or request difference.

The mechanism is outside the provider's own conversion code.  For a natively bound column msolap
hands the cell - held as a VARIANT - to the OLE DB data-conversion library,
`oledb32!IDataConvert` (CLSID_OLEDB_CONVERSIONLIBRARY).  Bracketing the failing call with two
`OutputDebugStringA` markers and running with `WINEDEBUG=+relay` shows, in the window a successful
read keeps empty:

    fixme:oledb:convert_DataConvert ...
    Unimplemented conversion 000c -> I8

and for the boolean column `Unimplemented conversion 000c -> BOOL`.  In `dlls/oledb32/convert.c`,
`convert_DataConvert` has a `case DBTYPE_VARIANT:` source for every destination it can reach
(I1/I2/I4/R4/R8/DATE/DBDATE/DBTIMESTAMP/BSTR/UI4/UI8/GUID/VARIANT/...), but not for `DBTYPE_I8` and
`DBTYPE_BOOL`, which therefore fall through to their `default:` arm and return `E_NOTIMPL`.
`convert_CanConvert`, on the other hand, advertises both conversions as supported - it classifies a
VARIANT source with BSTR and answers `S_OK` for any destination in `common_class()`, which contains
I8 and BOOL - so the provider's own capability check agrees with the platform's implementation and
the conversion is attempted.  The provider then reports the failed conversion as a failed row read,
which is the `E_BADACCESSOR` the client sees.  Windows' conversion library implements both
conversions, which is why the same read succeeds there.

The fix fills those two holes the way the neighbouring cases already do, with `VariantChangeType`
into the destination type (which is also what the documented behaviour of a VARIANT source is):

    case DBTYPE_VARIANT:
        VariantInit(&tmp);
        if ((hr = VariantChangeType(&tmp, src, 0, VT_I8)) == S_OK)
            *d = V_I8(&tmp);
        break;

(`VT_BOOL`/`V_BOOL` for the boolean destination).  The two conversions are the whole of the
failure: the relay window of a failing read contains no other conversion request, and the
provider's own relay window for a successful `UI8`/`I4` read differs from it in nothing else.

Verified two ways.

1. The conversion call on its own, with no engine and no provider (`tools/winapi/dataconvprobe.c`,
   loading `oledb32.dll` and asking for `IDataConvert`), against two builds of the same tree in two
   prefixes - `wine/install-clean` (pristine) and `wine/install` (this patch):

       source                -> destination     CanConvert   DataConvert (pristine)   DataConvert (patched)
       VARIANT(VT_I4)        -> DBTYPE_I8       0x00000000   0x80004001 E_NOTIMPL     S_OK, 1
       VARIANT(VT_I8)        -> DBTYPE_I8       0x00000000   0x80004001               S_OK, 1234567890123
       VARIANT(VT_BOOL)      -> DBTYPE_BOOL     0x00000000   0x80004001               S_OK, VARIANT_TRUE
       VARIANT(VT_I4)        -> DBTYPE_BOOL     0x00000000   0x80004001               S_OK, VARIANT_TRUE
       VARIANT(VT_I4=0)      -> DBTYPE_BOOL     0x00000000   0x80004001               S_OK, VARIANT_FALSE
       VARIANT(VT_I4)        -> I4/UI4/UI8/I1, VARIANT(VT_R8) -> R8: S_OK on both builds (unchanged)

   The failing side reports `DBSTATUS_E_BADACCESSOR` with `dst_len = 0` - the status the provider's
   client sees - while `CanConvert` says `S_OK`, which is the mismatch being fixed.

2. End to end through the provider and a live engine, `--native --app-props --exec-iid multi
   --fetch 1000`:

       EVALUATE ROW("i8", 1)   IRowset::GetData -> 0x80040E21  ->  0x00000000, row[0]: [i8]=1
       EVALUATE ROW("b", 1=1)  IRowset::GetData -> 0x80040E21  ->  0x00000000, row[0]: [b]=-1

   and the report that produced the app's error now renders its combo chart
   (`docs/I8_BINDING_FIX.md`).
--- a/dlls/oledb32/convert.c
+++ b/dlls/oledb32/convert.c
@@ -348,6 +348,11 @@
         {
         case DBTYPE_BSTR:        hr = VarI8FromStr(*(WCHAR**)src, LOCALE_USER_DEFAULT, 0, d); break;
         case DBTYPE_I8:          *d = *(LONGLONG*)src; hr = S_OK;              break;
+        case DBTYPE_VARIANT:
+            VariantInit(&tmp);
+            if ((hr = VariantChangeType(&tmp, src, 0, VT_I8)) == S_OK)
+                *d = V_I8(&tmp);
+            break;
         default: FIXME("Unimplemented conversion %04x -> I8\n", src_type); return E_NOTIMPL;
         }
         break;
@@ -434,6 +439,11 @@
         case DBTYPE_BSTR:        hr = VarBoolFromStr(*(WCHAR**)src, LOCALE_USER_DEFAULT, 0, d); break;
         case DBTYPE_BOOL:        *d = *(VARIANT_BOOL*)src; hr = S_OK;            break;
         case DBTYPE_DECIMAL:     hr = VarBoolFromDec(src, d);                    break;
+        case DBTYPE_VARIANT:
+            VariantInit(&tmp);
+            if ((hr = VariantChangeType(&tmp, src, 0, VT_BOOL)) == S_OK)
+                *d = V_BOOL(&tmp);
+            break;
         default: FIXME("Unimplemented conversion %04x -> BOOL\n", src_type); return E_NOTIMPL;
         }
         break;
